        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix: workspace divergence no longer wedges cloud-worker sessions",
        "body": "Related: #110224 (phase 2 of 3; phase 1 was #110952)\n\n## What Problem This Solves\n\nResolves the remaining \"unrecoverable session\" half of #110224: when the Gateway worktree diverged from the recorded base (for example the operator edited or ran files the cloud worker also changed), inbound reconciliation fail-stopped the whole turn. The reply streamed but the file result was refused, the turn claim stayed held so follow-up messages failed with an opaque \"already has an active turn claim\", and the recovery sweep retried the identical strict comparison forever — there was no way to resolve the conflict.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nInbound apply is now a three-way, keep-local-on-conflict reconciliation over the staged result tree from phase 1: paths where local still matches the base take the cloud version; paths already identical to the cloud version are no-ops; genuinely diverged paths (including worker-deleted-but-locally-modified and local-only collisions) keep the local version and are recorded as conflicts. A conflicted apply completes the turn normally — the base manifest is recomputed from the actual resulting workspace, the fence is accepted, and the claim releases — while the conflict list is reported in the session transcript (\"Cloud result applied with N conflict(s)… Cloud versions staged at <ref>\") and exposed on the placement projection for UI consumption. The staged ref of a conflicted apply is retained for inspection until its fence is removed. Turn-claim collisions now wait for release (existing 15s admission drain) before failing with an actionable message instead of throwing instantly. No new config, no schema changes; keep-local is the fixed policy (never silently overwrite operator edits).\n\n## User Impact\n\nA diverged workspace no longer wedges a cloud session: turns complete, conflicts are explicit and inspectable, Stop cloud worker works, and the transient claim error is replaced by a short wait and a clear message. Combined with #110952 this closes the operator-recovery gap in #110224 (phase 3 adds UI affordances).\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Remote (Blacksmith Testbox) full worker-environments suite: 90 files, 1179 tests passed. Post-rebase focused proof: gateway 103 tests + protocol 16 tests green.\n- New coverage: full three-way matrix (files, symlinks, additions, deletions, structural file/directory replacements, local-only collisions, non-portable nodes), claim release after conflicted apply, base-manifest recompute, staged-ref retention on conflict, wait-then-actionable claim collision, recovery sweep resolving a previously wedged fence with conflicts, journal replay guard when a conflicted apply reproduces the base hash.\n- Structured autoreview (codex, gpt-5.6-sol) on the isolated branch: clean, \"patch is correct\".\n",
